{% from 'networkpolicy.yml.j2' import networkpolicy with context %}
---
# NOTE: this role's OTHER policy file, networkpolicy-flaresolverr.yaml.j2, fences flaresolverr and
# is unrelated to this one. Both are applied.
#
# This rule is PORT-SCOPED: the baseline admits none of monitor-bridge, sonarr, radarr or
# prowlarr-verify, so they reach prowlarr only through this rule and only on this one port.
#
# TRAEFIK IS LISTED DELIBERATELY — do not remove it as redundant. While prowlarr carries the
# baseline label the union is the same with or without it. The entry matters for a pod that
# lacks the label, which this policy alone selects, and it keeps this policy's caller set
# complete if the baseline's traefik rule is ever port-scoped.
#
# sonarr and radarr reach prowlarr by bare name from their own application databases — the indexer
# definitions live on their PVCs, not in this repo.
#
# prowlarr-verify is the CONTROL leg of this role's OWN isolation check, the Job in
# templates/verify/ that Flux runs in the `prowlarr-verify` Kustomization: before it asserts
# flaresolverr is unreachable from outside prowlarr, it proves its own pod networking works by
# dialling prowlarr on this port. Fence that leg out and the control can never pass, so the Job
# fails on every revision — a negative test with no working control proves nothing. Same shape as
# k8s/roles/registry/templates/networkpolicy.yaml.j2 admitting its own selftest Jobs. Do not
# "clean this up" as an unused caller. It must never join flaresolverr's fence, which the same Job
# proves refuses it.
#
# The caller list is `netpol_from:` on this role's containers_list entry (cluster/services.yml),
# each caller's reason beside it. `netpol_role_owned: true` keeps netpol-baseline from
# rendering a second copy.
{{ networkpolicy(container_item.name, container_item.port, container_item | netpol_callers) }}
